  "id": "down_east_old_time_fiddle",
  "name": "Down East old-time fiddling (Canadian Maritime dance-band style)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Canadian old-time dance fiddling spread from the Maritimes by the New Brunswick-born fiddler Don Messer, whose band the Islanders broadcast from Charlottetown radio from 1939, recorded for the Apex label and reached a national audience on CBC radio and on the Halifax-produced CBC television show Don Messer's Jubilee until its cancellation in 1969. The style plays reels, jigs, hornpipes, breakdowns, waltzes and two-steps with a clean, lightly ornamented melody in strict dance tempo over a small band of piano, bass, guitar and drums, and it became a standard of Canadian old-time fiddling and its contests.",
  "recipe": "classic North American late-1930s down east old-time fiddling, functional Song, pre commercial branch. fiddle, upright piano, upright bass, dreadnought acoustic guitar, drum kit. hardwood living. passive ribbon, tube pre, German tube console. Studer 30ips tape.",
